<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1351">1351</a>–<a href="/years/1402">1402</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gian Galeazzo Visconti ruled part of the Milanese lands from 1378, seized sole power from his uncle Bernabò in 1385 and in 1395 bought from King Wenceslaus the title of Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. He conquered Verona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, Siena, Perugia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and seemed close to dominating northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> when he died of plague in 1402. He began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-cathedral">Milan Cathedral</a> and the Certosa of Pavia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti.jpg" alt="Gian Galeazzo Visconti" width="310" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Giovanni Ambrogio de Predis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GiovAmbrogiodePredisattribGianGaleazzoVisconti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gian Galeazzo Visconti was born on 16 October 1351, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-ii-visconti">Galeazzo II Visconti</a>, who held the signoria of Pavia, and Bianca of Savoy. His first marriage was to Isabelle of Valois, who brought him the title of comte de Vertus in Champagne, rendered in Italian as Conte di Virtù, the name by which he was known early in his career. After she died in childbirth in 1372, he married his cousin Caterina Visconti, daughter of Bernabò, on 2 October 1380. His daughter Valentina, by Isabelle, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans">Louis I, Duke of Orléans</a>, on 17 August 1389.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1385 Gian Galeazzo gained control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> from his uncle Bernabò, with whom he had ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> jointly. According to the account given, he faked a religious conversion and ambushed him during a procession. Bernabò was imprisoned and soon died, supposedly poisoned on Gian Galeazzo's orders. He then took Verona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, and controlled almost the whole Po Valley, including Piacenza, though Padua reverted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-novello-da-carrara">Francesco Novello da Carrara</a> in 1390. In 1395 King Wenceslaus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> gave him the title Duke of Milan for 100,000 florins.</p><p>He spent 300,000 gold florins trying to turn the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mincio">Mincio</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brenta">Brenta</a> from their courses to leave Mantua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> helpless. In 1400 he appointed clerks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> for public health and, with his chancellor Francesco Barbavara, is credited with an efficient and innovative bureaucracy. He completed the Visconti Castle at Pavia, begun by his father, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/certosa-di-pavia">Certosa di Pavia</a> and furthered work on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>'s Duomo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gian Galeazzo angered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by seeking to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> from French sovereignty, and when Queen Isabeau's faction spread rumours about Valentina and she was exiled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1396, he threatened war. After the French-led crusade met disaster at Nicopolis, he was strongly suspected of having told the Turks its strength and plans.</p><p>He dreamed of uniting northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as a revived Lombard empire, and in 1402 he attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, which had joined the League of Bologna; he won a victory over the Bolognese at Casalecchio on 26–27 June 1402. He fell ill at the castle of Melegnano and died on 3 September 1402. His lands were divided among his legitimate and illegitimate children, who fought each other, and the territories he had ruled broke apart.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gian Galeazzo Visconti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gian Galeazzo Visconti</b> did not inherit his ducal title: he bought it in 1395 from Wenceslas, King of the Romans, for a sum reported as 100,000 florins.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Florence fought the papacy in the War of the Eight Saints (1375–1378) and survived the expansion of <b>Giangaleazzo Visconti</b> of Milan, whose death in 1402 relieved the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gian Galeazzo Visconti</b> overthrew his uncle Bernabò in 1385 and created a centralised state, beginning Milan Cathedral in 1386.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-milan">Lordship of Milan</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gian Galeazzo Visconti</b> took Padua in 1388, but Francesco Novello regained it in 1390.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-padua">Lordship of Padua</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1387 <b>Gian Galeazzo Visconti</b> seized Verona from Antonio della Scala and annexed it to the Lordship of Milan.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-verona">Lordship of Verona</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1399 Gherardo Appiani, lord of Pisa, sold the city to <b>Gian Galeazzo Visconti</b> but kept Piombino, Elba and nearby islands as his own lordship.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-piombino">Principality of Piombino</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pavia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Melegnano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.19,9.16&amp;year=1384" aria-label="Pavia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 569 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gian Galeazzo Visconti"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 839h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 839h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.19,9.16&amp;year=1384">What was here in 1384? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
